#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147149 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "39259a31-5d67-5482-b1bb-4fa719ce4f95" :question/math-academy-id "e-20894" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$-\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\textrm{d}x + Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $Q(x,y)$?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{1}{x}$\n2. $\\dfrac{y}{x}$\n3. $e^{yx}$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's differentiate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{x^2}\\right)=-\\dfrac{1}{x^2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nto each of the given options and determine whether the results match up with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\n$$\n\nabove:\n\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$, then we get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac 1 x\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2} = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- If $Q(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$, then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(\\dfrac y x\\right) = -\\dfrac{y}{x^2} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n- If $Q(x,y) = e^{xy}$, then we do *not* get an exact equation:\n$\\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(e^{xy}\\right) = ye^{xy} \\neq \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cbd51b4-cbd1-5e18-8c49-44be7967cd38" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exact Differential Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "ca703f4f-5363-5fc1-8372-136e7c87156d" :question/math-academy-id "e-20895" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$(y^2-2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (2xy +1)\\textrm{d}y = 0$." :question/worked-solution "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = y^2-2x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y) = 2xy+1\n$$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫P(x, y)dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= ∫y^{2} - 2xdx + f(y) \\\\\n&= xy^{2} - x^{2} + f(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫Q(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= ∫2xy + 1dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= xy^{2} + y + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = -x^2, \\qquad f(y) = y\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\nxy^2-x^2 + y = c\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147152 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df3a798c-f226-5b4f-8d65-4a305de2b087" :knowledge-point/title "Checking ODEs for Exactness"}] :question/id #uuid "36440ae0-1cee-52c0-b24a-7871e172a341" :question/math-academy-id "e-20933" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is exact?\n\n1. $(2xy)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n2. $(y^{2}+2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\,\\textrm{d}y = 0$\n3. $(7y - x)\\,\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\,\\textrm{d}x = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation:\n\n- The first equation is\n$(2xy)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (x^{2}+4)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = 2xy, \\qquad Q(x,y) = x^{2}+4$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy); = 2x$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + 4); = 2x$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\nThus, equation I is exact.\n- The second equation is\n$(y^{2}+2x)\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xy+1)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = y^{2}+2x, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xy+1$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + 2x); = 2y$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy + 1); = y$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {\\neq} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\nThus, equation II is not exact.\n- The third equation is\n$(7y - x)\\;\\textrm{d}y - (y - 3x)\\;\\textrm{d}x = 0$.\nTherefore, the functions $P$ and $Q$ are\n$P(x,y) = -(y - 3x) = 3x - y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 7y - x$.\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x - y); =-1$\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y - x); =-1$\nSo,\n$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\nThus, equation III is exact.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147153 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7bb08d6e-ae86-50f5-8cb4-d7fe50595849" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging an ODE Into Exact Form and Then Solving"}] :question/id #uuid "c1b768cb-8709-55f0-878b-d9608ac1c478" :question/math-academy-id "e-20934" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the differential equation\n$y' = \\frac{11+ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}$." :question/worked-solution "We start by rewriting this equation in differential form.\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nStarting from\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= \\frac{dy}{dx} \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{11 + ye^{xy}}{13y - xe^{xy}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe first multiply through by $(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x$:\n\n$$\n(13y-xe^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}y = (11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we then rearrange the terms:\n\n$$\n(11+ye^{xy})\\;\\textrm{d}x + (xe^{xy}-13y)\\;\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we should check for exactness. A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\textrm{d}x +Q(x,y)\\textrm{d}y = 0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\n$$\n\nFor our differential equation, we have\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =11+ye^{xy}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = xe^{xy}-13y\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(11 + ye^{xy}) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} + xye^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xe^{xy} - 13y) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} + xye^{xy}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} {=} \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}. \\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is exact.\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial x} = P(x,y),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y} = Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫P(x, y)dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= ∫(11 + ye^{xy})dx + f(y) \\\\\n&= 11x + e^{xy} + f(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu(x, y) &= ∫Q(x, y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= ∫(xe^{xy} - 13y)dy + g(x) \\\\\n&= e^{xy} - \\frac{13}{2}y^{2} + g(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x) = 11x, \\quad f(y) =-\\dfrac{13}{2}y^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y) = c$ is\n\n$$\ne^{xy} - \\dfrac{13}{2}y^2 + 11x = c\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ed6eeb5-a646-5f47-b6d8-f1078f550376" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Factors Giving an Exact ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "987105b6-6efc-548a-92a2-82bd1f6712c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146462} {:answer/id #uuid "a8d86731-7aed-5f2b-96a0-f421bf69d7d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146463} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e4213a-c93e-5899-bc48-e492bfbcbf17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146464} {:answer/id #uuid "376cc1ab-793a-5c84-81c4-714df491f3c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146465} {:answer/id #uuid "54f9aa97-dc86-50c1-8691-697c9bb46a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "987105b6-6efc-548a-92a2-82bd1f6712c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146462} :answer-field/id #uuid "f83f97ce-cbc9-58c2-bc53-a272b5b0ea58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146461}] :question/id #uuid "3c92b66b-ccec-5e6d-8f75-9e3b9cdb1f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-64207" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation:\n\n$P(x, y)dx + 4y^{3}dy = 0$\n\nGiven that the differential equation is exact, which of the following functions could be $P(x, y)$?\n\n1. $\\cos (x) + 8$\n2. $\\ln (x + 1) - 2$\n3. $2x + 7$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4cbd51b4-cbd1-5e18-8c49-44be7967cd38" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exact Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9966ebf8-0f5f-51a6-9dc3-149517057acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} - y^{2} =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146468} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a17b25-4ef2-58b9-879a-fde82be35538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy + e^{xy} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146469} {:answer/id #uuid "48583d79-14b8-5ccf-87a3-80d948b8bed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + e^{xy} = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146470} {:answer/id #uuid "655eb48e-cc75-5f2e-b8b3-0309d188e190" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + e^{xy} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146471} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1cc4a5-4fe4-5d2d-87f9-ba991a15cc19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + xy + e^{y} = e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e1cc4a5-4fe4-5d2d-87f9-ba991a15cc19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x + xy + e^{y} = e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146472} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c52da4a-6e18-5c3b-b8f6-6eda4428aaba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146467}] :question/id #uuid "9efa7792-8965-523c-b567-7789ca6c3aa3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64215" :question/problem "Find the solution of the exact differential equation\n\n$(4 + y)dx + (x + e^{y})dy = 0$\n\nthat satisfies the initial condition $y(0) = 1$."}]}
